# The six safety instructions this site sends to a browser.
#
# WHAT A SAFETY INSTRUCTION IS. When a browser asks for a page, the server can
# send short instructions alongside it telling the browser how to behave. They
# are called response headers. Each one below switches off a way a site can be
# attacked. Nothing here changes how the page looks.
#
# WHERE THIS FILE IS READ. Every modern static host reads a file named _headers
# at the top of the site and sends what it says: Cloudflare Pages, Netlify and
# the rest all use this exact format. Porkbun, which serves arkhimedes.ai today,
# does not read it, and that is proved rather than assumed by
# scripts/ask-the-live-site-for-the-six.mjs, which fetches the real address and
# prints what actually arrived. The two that a browser will obey when they come
# from inside the page itself are ALSO written into index.html as meta tags, so
# they work today. The file is here so that the day the site moves to a host
# that reads it, all six arrive with no further work.

/*
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains; preload
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; img-src 'self' data:; style-src 'unsafe-inline'; font-src data:; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: accelerometer=(), camera=(), geolocation=(), gyroscope=(), magnetometer=(), microphone=(), payment=(), usb=(), interest-cohort=()
